---
import Legal from '../../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../config';
---

<Legal
  lang="en"
  page="privacy"
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="Your financial records in Tanukonomy are stored on your device, not on our servers. Technical data and the Smart Record text sent to Google are explained here."
>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>In short:</strong> everything you record is stored on your device and is not sent to us. What leaves the device is
    limited technical data (updates, usage statistics, crash reports) and, if you use Smart Record, text that Google
    interprets. Details are in section 4.
  </p>

  <p>
    This policy explains how the Tanukonomy app (“the app”) and the tanukonomy.app website (“the site”) handle information.
    “We” means the developer of Tanukonomy.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Data you record in the app</h2>
  <p>
    The app stores the data you enter yourself: wallets and balances, transactions (amount, date, category, note), budgets,
    freelance projects and hours, and app settings. This data:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>is stored <strong>only in your device’s local storage</strong>;</li>
    <li>
      is not sent to us. The only thing that can reach a third party is text you ask the app to interpret through Smart
      Record, as described in section 4;
    </li>
    <li>cannot be viewed, restored, or deleted remotely by us.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. What we don’t collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>There is no connection to banks, e-wallets, or payment services, and we never ask for passwords.</li>
    <li>There are no ads in the app.</li>
    <li>There is no access to your location, contacts, or camera.</li>
    <li>
      No stored wallets, transactions, budgets, or freelance projects are sent anywhere. Smart Record text (section 4) is
      the only exception.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Account (optional)</h2>
  <p>
    The app is fully functional without an account. If you choose to sign in, we receive your account’s email address and
    user ID to manage that account. You can sign in with a Google account or with an email and password. An account does
    not cause your financial data to be sent anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Technical data and third-party services</h2>
  <p>Besides the account, the app uses the services below. None of them receives the financial records stored in the app.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Firebase Analytics and Crashlytics (Google).</strong> The app sends basic usage events and crash reports so
      we can improve it. This runs for all users, with or without an account. Update-related events include the type of
      update banner, the action you choose (for example Update or Later), and the build number and small-update number. The
      small-update number is also recorded as a single attribute on events and crash reports.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Firebase Remote Config (Google).</strong> The app uses it to fetch the “What’s new” release notes. Its SDK
      automatically sends the country code, language code, time zone, platform and operating system version, Firebase
      Android App ID, package name, SDK version, and a Firebase installation ID. That ID is created per app installation and
      does not uniquely identify a user or a device.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Small updates through Shorebird.</strong> The app contacts Shorebird’s server to ask whether a small update is
      available. The data sent is an anonymous installation ID that Shorebird generates at random (used to count active
      installations), the app id, the release and build version, the small-update number, the platform, and the processor
      architecture. There is no location, device fingerprint, advertising ID, or user data. Like any internet service,
      Shorebird’s server operational logs may include an IP address. Their official description is in the
      <a href="https://docs.shorebird.dev/code-push/faq/" rel="noopener">Shorebird FAQ</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Version updates through Google Play.</strong> The app uses Google Play’s in-app updates service. The version
      check is performed by Google Play services on your device. The app sends no data to us or to our servers for this.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Voice recording.</strong> This feature asks for microphone permission. Your speech is turned into text by a
      speech recognition service. If the offline language pack is not installed, that service may use Google’s servers. The
      app does not store voice recordings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Smart Record with Gemini (Google, through Firebase AI Logic).</strong> When the on-device rules are unsure how
      to interpret a piece of text (spoken text or notification text), the app sends that text to Gemini to turn it into a
      draft transaction. What is sent is that text, the names of your active wallets and categories, and the date. Balances,
      transaction history, internal IDs, and the name of the app a notification came from are not sent. In notification
      text, numbers that are not the transaction amount, such as account numbers and balances, are masked before sending.
      The text sent can still contain amounts or names you speak, so avoid saying information you do not want to share.
      Spoken text is not stored after the draft is created. Google’s processing is subject to the Gemini API terms. If you
      are offline or the request fails, the app uses the on-device interpretation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Record from notifications (Android, optional).</strong> If you turn it on, you grant notification access and
      choose which apps are read. Only notifications from the apps you pick that pass the transaction filter are processed.
      The text is kept on your device for at most 7 days.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These services are run by their providers and are subject to their policies. Details of the data the Firebase SDKs send
    are in the
    <a href="https://firebase.google.com/docs/android/play-data-disclosure" rel="noopener">Firebase data disclosure</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Deleting your data</h2>
  <p>
    Because your financial data lives only on your device, you are fully in control. Uninstalling the app, or clearing its
    data in Android settings, permanently deletes all records. We keep no copy, so deleted data cannot be recovered by us.
    If you use an account, you can delete it from inside the app or through the
    <a href="https://tanukonomy.app/en/delete-account" rel="noopener">delete account page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Device backups</h2>
  <p>
    If your operating system’s built-in backup (such as Google backup on Android) is turned on, the system may include app
    data in your backup. Those backups are managed by the operating system provider under their policies, not by us.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Closed beta</h2>
  <p>
    To join the closed beta, you join a Google Group and the Google Play testing program with your Google account. Both are
    run by Google. As the group manager, we can see members’ email addresses; we use them only to grant beta access and do
    not share them. Feedback you send by email or through Google Play is used only to improve the app.
  </p>

  <h2>8. This site</h2>
  <p>
    This site uses no cookies, forms, or analytics. The hosting provider may log standard technical data such as IP address
    and browser type for security and operations, under their own policies.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Children</h2>
  <p>The app is not directed at children under 13.</p>

  <h2>10. Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If the app later adds a feature that changes how data is handled (such as sync), we will update this page and its
    effective date before that feature becomes available.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Contact</h2>
  <p>Privacy questions can be sent to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
</Legal>
